WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.235
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:10.670
So I want you to think back on
if you've received an email

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:17.741
from a political campaign that read
something like this or this or this.

00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:27.630
So this is what my inbox looked like,
last week and I'll come clean.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.640
Having worked on a campaign,
I've sent 1000s of these emails.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:37.822
A belated apology if any of you watching
are from the state of New Hampshire.

00:00:37.822 --> 00:00:40.237
Now, it may come as a surprise,

00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:45.350
to hear that emails are a major part
of how candidates reach voters.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.350
However, I'm sure it's no surprise to
hear that they're incredibly ineffective.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.950
Click through rates are abysmal.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:00.613
And studies have shown that excess
outreach actually reduces intent to vote,

00:01:00.613 --> 00:01:02.680
it's counterproductive.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.230
However, on a campaign,
there's no time to experiment.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.540
No time to consider new strategies.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.542
We do what's been done before.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:20.310
In fact, over the last two decades,
the playbook for

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.550
driving voter turn out has
not significantly changed.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.590
We've just gone from donor thing
to bar worthy vision spam.

00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:35.190
And yet working on campaigns, are baffled.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.480
Given the billions spent on
elections every four years.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.940
How do we not have a better system for
voter outreach?

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.187
Or even more broadly,

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:53.900
why does it seem that the issues
plaguing our democracy are unended?

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.896
The lack of secure voting, disinformation,

00:01:59.896 --> 00:02:03.700
partisan districting, I could go on.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.845
So this is what I'm here
to talk about today.

00:02:08.845 --> 00:02:13.709
1, what's wrong with our democracy, 2,

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:19.982
why democracy entrepreneurship
might be the solution and

00:02:19.982 --> 00:02:24.602
3, how we can fund and support this work?

00:02:24.602 --> 00:02:27.168
So let's start with the first question.

00:02:27.168 --> 00:02:28.630
What's wrong with our democracy?

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.090
Most Americans once acknowledged
a few universal truths.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.690
The sun will rise,
Tom Brady will enter the Super Bowl.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.630
And American democracy will persist.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.580
Now fast forward to 2022.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.475
Tom Brady's retired and
according to a recent poll,

00:02:52.475 --> 00:02:58.150
64% of Americans I believe that
US democracy is in crisis.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.750
Now, this is a harrowing fact.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.839
Our democracy is the system by which
we peacefully transition power and

00:03:07.839 --> 00:03:09.660
hold leaders accountable.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:16.082
It is because of the resiliency of
our democracy, that we have free and

00:03:16.082 --> 00:03:20.930
fair markets and why the US dollar
is the world's reserve currency.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.468
Without a trusted government,
we cannot fight corruption,

00:03:27.468 --> 00:03:30.910
advocate for human rights or
pursue justice.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.689
These are the promises that
a healthy democracy delivers.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:43.130
Thus, it is baffling to think that we
are not only ignoring improvement,

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.910
but we're actively moving backwards.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.636
The last time trust levels in
the American democratic process

00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:58.207
were this low was in the 1850s
in the lead up to the civil war.

00:03:58.207 --> 00:04:02.519
The nonpartisan electoral
integrity project ranks

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:07.713
the United States second to last
among liberal democracies and

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:12.918
57 overall, in administering trusted and
fair elections.

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:16.921
We must completely reject
the notion that our democracy will

00:04:16.921 --> 00:04:19.439
magically bumble into better times.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.039
It won't without active intervention.

00:04:25.256 --> 00:04:29.751
So, with a laundry list of issues in tow,
you'd guess a country like

00:04:29.751 --> 00:04:33.705
the United States, so
focused on relentless improvement,

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:37.600
would throw its full weight
into revitalizing democracy.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.641
However government isn't civilized
to tackle democracy reform.

00:04:43.641 --> 00:04:46.357
And I can think of a few reasons why.

00:04:46.357 --> 00:04:52.299
1, it's hard to use the system
to change the system.

00:04:52.299 --> 00:04:57.139
Politicians aren't civilized to open
the process that puts them in power.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:04.885
2, dependency corruption,
the constant need to fundraise for

00:05:04.885 --> 00:05:09.705
elections often drives
partisanship over progress.

00:05:09.705 --> 00:05:14.853
3, polarization, and
trench polarization makes any

00:05:14.853 --> 00:05:20.118
chance a passing by partisan
electoral reform difficult

00:05:21.565 --> 00:05:26.577
And 4, the scale of government,
even donors who are passionate

00:05:26.577 --> 00:05:31.880
about democracy, think they can
just support perform candidates.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:38.204
However, reform from the politicians
cannot unilaterally make progress.

00:05:40.031 --> 00:05:46.113
So because government isn't
incentivized to tackle this issue,

00:05:46.113 --> 00:05:52.094
we need to do what America does best,
turn to our entrepreneurs.

00:05:52.094 --> 00:05:55.749
In this case,
I'm talking about democracy entrepreneurs.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:01.990
Democracy entrepreneurs represent a legion
of passionate and patriotic leaders.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:08.475
They include technologists,
they include research organizations,

00:06:08.475 --> 00:06:13.082
they include community organizers and
they include advocacy

00:06:13.082 --> 00:06:17.970
groups that drive innovation and
change in a space that needs it.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:24.326
It is because of democracy entrepreneurs,
at ballot ready that we have access to

00:06:24.326 --> 00:06:27.750
state and local level ballot
information with two clicks.

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:34.018
It's because of democracy entrepreneurs
at Rock the Vote that we can register to

00:06:34.018 --> 00:06:34.900
vote online.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.189
It's because of democracy
entrepreneurs at Politifact that we

00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:43.230
have near instant fact
checking of politicians.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.031
Whereas private sector entrepreneurs
innovate in antiquated industries and

00:06:50.031 --> 00:06:54.253
social entrepreneurs take on
issues tackling societal safety.

00:06:54.253 --> 00:06:58.831
Democracy entrepreneurs
are working on solutions to build

00:06:58.831 --> 00:07:03.600
a government of the people,
by the people and for the people.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.810
Their key distinction is that they're
not chasing a financial exit.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.039
They're often nonprofits or
social welfare organizations.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.713
So we've laid out
the issues of democracy and

00:07:18.713 --> 00:07:21.920
identified who is best
position to fix them.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:29.002
So now to address the last question,
how we can fund and support this work?

00:07:30.998 --> 00:07:35.756
To best illustrate this, I'll share
a story about a time that I worked for

00:07:35.756 --> 00:07:40.287
a democracy entrepreneur at Vote Rev.
we used behavioral science and

00:07:40.287 --> 00:07:44.150
organizing expertise to drive
bipartisan voter turnout.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.780
It was September of 2020 and
our organization was unstoppable.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.210
We'd raised over $10 million.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.530
We'd recently doubled our headcount.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.519
And we were actively turning
out thousands of early voters.

00:07:59.519 --> 00:08:06.125
Now fast forward to February 2021,
exactly one month after the run offs.

00:08:06.125 --> 00:08:09.968
We were no longer
receiving contributions and

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:13.919
over 50% of my colleagues
had been laid off.

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:18.044
The core issue is that
infusions of political dollars

00:08:18.044 --> 00:08:20.710
are painfully tied to elections.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.350
Every 4 years, candidates and political
groups are flooded with billions.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:32.385
And like clockwork post elections,
those dollars dry up.

00:08:32.385 --> 00:08:38.040
No matter how successful we were in 2020,
we couldn't raise our series B.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.950
We had to downsize just like hundreds
of other democracy ventures.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.580
And just like that,
progress was ground to a halt.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.873
And our democracy remained stuck,
waiting for the inevitable,

00:08:51.873 --> 00:08:55.810
another election, for
those funds to come flooding back in.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.480
So, this is the fate of democracy
entrepreneurship today.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.946
Blessed and dammed by the whims of donors,

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:11.902
tied to the shoe strings of
political contributions and

00:09:11.902 --> 00:09:16.869
wholly dependent on
the cyclicality of our elections.

00:09:16.869 --> 00:09:20.854
So how do we fix this.

00:09:20.854 --> 00:09:25.491
The first part of the solution involves
reallocating political dollars to fund

00:09:25.491 --> 00:09:27.160
democracy entrepreneurs.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:33.780
Every 4 years donors give over $10 billion
to campaigns and political groups.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:37.488
And only a fraction of a percent of
that money goes towards strengthening

00:09:37.488 --> 00:09:38.170
democracy.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.822
Just like we do with science and
infrastructure,

00:09:42.822 --> 00:09:45.517
we need to make more
investments in democracy and

00:09:45.517 --> 00:09:48.690
we need to make them more
strategically and longer term.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.600
The second part of the solution
involves supporting this work.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:58.587
This is where you all come in as
future investors and operators,

00:09:58.587 --> 00:10:03.645
we need to empower this work by building
accelerators and incubators by cementing

00:10:03.645 --> 00:10:08.140
democracy innovation, as a part of
corporate social responsibility.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.984
By providing technical
resources to founders and

00:10:12.984 --> 00:10:15.780
by evangelizing to build the movement.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:21.320
I am calling for
a pro mass democracy mobilization event.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:26.886
It's about time we recognize the service
of democracy entrepreneurs and

00:10:26.886 --> 00:10:29.180
flood them with funding and support.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:35.581
We do this with the goal of inviting all
Americans to co-create our nation by

00:10:35.581 --> 00:10:39.350
giving them a fair say in
the forces that govern their lives.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:45.628
And I'll end with this,
it's not about which side wins,

00:10:45.628 --> 00:10:49.742
what matters most is for
American democracy to win.

00:10:49.742 --> 00:10:55.382
[MUSIC]

